<h1>How do you measure the ROI of an office redesign? The indicators that really matter</h1>
<p class="meta">Spaces That Matter — Blog &amp; Resources</p>
<p class="lead">Companies invest millions in their offices. They measure cost per square foot, occupancy rates, energy savings. But how do you measure what really matters — the impact on the people who work there? An analytical framework built on 5 indicators.</p>
<p>Every year, French companies spend several billion euros fitting out, renovating or relocating their workspaces. These decisions are often backed by solid arguments: optimizing floor space, modernizing the company's image, adapting to hybrid work.</p>
<p>But when it's time to take stock — 12 months after the grand opening, when leadership asks whether the investment was worth it — the answer is rarely satisfying. Out come the desk occupancy data, the satisfaction rate from the annual survey, sometimes absenteeism statistics. And the conclusion is that “things are going well”.</p>
<p>That isn't management. It's hope.</p>

<h2>The problem with traditional real estate indicators</h2>
<p>The indicators usually used to assess an office's “performance” are essentially real estate and operational metrics: desk occupancy rate, operating cost per square foot, energy savings achieved, number of meetings held in meeting rooms.</p>
<p>These indicators measure the efficiency of the space as physical infrastructure. They say nothing about the impact of that space on the people who use it. Yet that is where the real value of an office lies, at a time when talent is scarce and remote work is everywhere.</p>
<p>An office can have a 90% occupancy rate and a well-being score of 4/10. That means people come in — perhaps because they have to — but they are suffering. It is the opposite of a high-performing investment.</p>

<h2>The 5 human performance indicators to measure</h2>

<h2>#1. The office well-being score</h2>
<p>The first indicator is the most direct: to what extent do employees feel good in their workspace? This score, measured through a structured questionnaire, should cover the physical dimensions (comfort, acoustics, light), the psychological dimensions (sense of autonomy and safety) and the social dimensions (quality of relationships).</p>
<p>Benchmark: an office well-being score below 6.5/10 is associated with a 34% increase in the risk of disengagement (Source: Leesman Index, 2023).</p>

<h2>#2. The voluntary attendance rate</h2>
<p>Beyond the occupancy rate (how many people are present), the voluntary attendance rate measures the proportion of office days that are chosen rather than mandated. In hybrid organizations with genuine flexibility, this indicator reveals the perceived value of the office.</p>
<p>An office that draws its employees in voluntarily 3 days a week creates more value than a mandatory 5-day office where presenteeism masks deep disengagement.</p>

<h2>#3. The collaboration index</h2>
<p>This indicator measures the quality and quantity of meaningful interactions that take place in the workspace. It can be assessed through direct questions (“To what extent does your office encourage spontaneous encounters and exchanges with your colleagues?”) or, in a more sophisticated way, through movement-flow analysis tools.</p>
<p>The collaboration index is particularly relevant for assessing redesign projects meant to encourage teamwork — such as creating collaborative or social areas.</p>

<h2>#4. The concentration index</h2>
<p>The counterpart of the collaboration index, the concentration index measures the extent to which the office allows employees to focus deeply on their individual tasks. It is often underestimated in redesign projects — and yet the ability to concentrate deeply is a major predictor of productivity and job satisfaction.</p>
<p>In open-plan offices without quiet retreat areas, this index is almost always poor.</p>

<h2>#5. The belonging and meaning score</h2>
<p>The fifth indicator is more subtle — and yet fundamental. To what extent does the workspace strengthen the sense of belonging to the organization? To what extent does it reflect the company's values and culture?</p>
<p>This score is directly linked to engagement and retention. Studies show that employees with a strong sense of organizational belonging are 5 times less likely to look for another job (Deloitte Global Millennial Survey, 2022).</p>
<p>“The real ROI of an office isn't measured at the grand opening — it's measured 18 months later, in engagement and retention rates.”</p>

<h2>How to build these indicators into your management</h2>
<p>Putting these 5 indicators in place does not require complex technology infrastructure. It requires three things:</p>
<p>A structured employee questionnaire, administered before the redesign (T0), then 6 months (T1) and 12 months (T2) after. This is the only way to measure the real impact — and to isolate the effect of the redesign from other variables (organizational changes, changes in headcount, etc.).</p>
<p>A commitment from leadership to act on the data. A questionnaire with no follow-up is worse than no questionnaire at all — it creates expectations that end in disappointment. The “governance and participation” dimension of our 2024 barometer showed this clearly.</p>
<p>An external frame of reference. Your raw scores only make sense when compared with a norm. The STM framework provides this benchmark — letting you know whether your score of 6.2/10 on the acoustic dimension is average for your sector, below it, or ahead of it.</p>

<h2>A concrete example: before / after</h2>
<p>A Paris-based consulting firm (250 people) measured its spaces with the STM framework before moving into new offices, and 9 months afterwards. The results:</p>
<p>Overall score: from 5.3/10 to 7.1/10 (+1.8 points). Physical well-being: from 4.8 to 7.4 (a major gain thanks to acoustic solutions and natural light). Governance dimension: from 3.9 to 6.2 (project co-created with the teams). Share of voluntary office days: from 45% to 72%. Absenteeism: -18% over the period.</p>
<p>This is data that neither the occupancy rate nor the cost per square foot could have captured. And it is this data that enabled leadership to justify the investment — and to know what to improve in the next phase.</p>

<h2>Conclusion: investing without measuring is spending without knowing</h2>
<p>Office real estate is companies' second-largest cost item, after payroll. At a time when competition for talent is intensifying and the office has to justify itself against remote work, managing this investment with human performance indicators is no longer a luxury — it is a strategic necessity.</p>
<p>Spaces That Matter was built precisely to meet this need: to provide a rigorous, scientifically grounded and operationally useful framework for measuring what really matters in a workspace.</p>
